rationale:
structural: Every clause of `requirements.test-coverage.check.exceptions.setup` specifies what the generated documentation and bundled instructions must say. None prescribes how they are built.

- "explain how to write a standard `habeas:coverage` citation with a prose justification under {cite}`...anchor`" and "author or refresh its citation review using `habeas author`".
- "the anchor requires no project declaration or exception-specific configuration".
- "only current accepted citation evidence permits an exception".
- "ordinary partially uncovered functions need none when their file meets its target".

The clause on how to write the citation and its review names the commands and citation forms the text must explain. It does not require a library, type, signature or delegation. A different build of the same documents would still satisfy the text. For example, the guidance could be stored as a Markdown file instead of a Rust constant. It could be generated by different code, or placed in another bundled skill file. Any of these would produce the same words and still meet the text. So the requirement has no structural obligation that needs implementation evidence.

The supplied roots already carry the stated content:
- `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md#section[Habeas]/section[Testing%20and%20coverage%20artifacts]/paragraph@7ca17af232fd` covers every required point:
  - a standard `{cite}` of `habeas:coverage`;
  - rationale in fenced commentary or a Rust doc comment;
  - `habeas author`;
  - no project declaration or extra configuration;
  - only current accepted review evidence permits the exception;
  - partly covered functions need no exception when the file meets its target.
- `md/reference.md#section[How%20it%20works]/section[References]/section[Built-in%20definitions]/paragraph@d87e9cbd9da4` repeats the doc-comment form, the accepted-review rule, and the scope of file and function exceptions.

The setup-generated book text, `src/execution_coverage.rs#const[GUIDANCE]`, also states the same content. It is inserted by `src/execution_coverage/book.rs`. Wording is behavior, so no `{impl}` marker is needed there.

Test edges exist for the content: `tests/repository.rs#fn[shared_agent_guidance_documents_post_suite_checks_and_standard_explanations]`, `tests/setup.rs#fn[coverage_setup_is_idempotent_and_hook_needs_no_coverage_chapter]` and `src/execution_coverage/book.rs#mod[tests]/fn[generated_reports_inherit_the_requirements_index_hierarchy]`. I did not read their bodies, so I make no claim about whether they are adequate. That belongs to the behavioral criterion.

behavioral: The subject is a documentation-content requirement. It covers the generated coverage documentation and the bundled agent instructions. Each must explain five things: (a) a standard `habeas:coverage` citation with a prose justification, (b) authoring or refreshing its citation review with `habeas author`, (c) that the anchor needs no project declaration or exception-specific configuration, (d) that only current accepted citation evidence permits an exception, and (e) that ordinary partially uncovered functions need none when their file meets its target.

- **Generated coverage documentation.** `src/execution_coverage/book.rs#mod[tests]/fn[generated_reports_inherit_the_requirements_index_hierarchy]` builds the generated page through `populate_in`. It asserts that the page contains:
  - `habeas:coverage`;
  - "substantive prose justification";
  - "fenced commentary or a Rust doc comment";
  - "citation review with `habeas author`";
  - "current accepted review";
  - "No project declaration or special configuration";
  - "Ordinary partly covered functions need no exception when their file meets its target".

  This covers (a) to (e). It runs for both numbered and unnumbered parents.
- **Bundled agent instructions.** `tests/repository.rs#fn[shared_agent_guidance_documents_post_suite_checks_and_standard_explanations]` reads `assets/skills/habeas/SKILL.md` and asserts these phrases:
  - "standard `{cite}` of `habeas:coverage`";
  - "rationale in fenced commentary or a Rust doc comment";
  - "citation review with `habeas author`";
  - "Only current accepted review evidence permits the exception";
  - "No project declaration or extra configuration is needed";
  - "Partly covered functions need no exception when their file meets its target".

  This covers (a) to (e) for the bundled skill.
- **Setup.** `tests/setup.rs#fn[coverage_setup_is_idempotent_and_hook_needs_no_coverage_chapter]` is marked for this requirement. It shows setup writes no separate coverage chapter, so the generated report page and the bundled skill are the documents that carry the explanation. It adds no explanation assertions of its own.

The tests assert the required content as text, which is the only observable behaviour this requirement states. No behavioural obligation is left without an assertion. The word "refresh" is not asserted separately. It is part of the single "author or refresh" instruction, which the "citation review with `habeas author`" assertions cover.

